1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,060
As you all know, the university is planning an arts festival for later this year,

2
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:14,390
and here in the music department we've planned three concerts.

3
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:21,650
These will be public performances, and the programme has just been finalised.

4
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:28,240
The theme of the festival is links between the UK and Australia

5
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,659
and this is reflected in the music:

6
00:00:31,659 --> 00:00:36,400
each concert will feature both British and Australian composers.

7
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:41,020
I'll tell you briefly about the Australian music

8
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:44,180
as you probably won't be familiar with that.

9
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:49,800
The first concert will include music by Liza Lim

10
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:55,460
who was born in Perth, Western Australia, in 1966.

11
00:00:55,460 --> 00:01:00,640
As a child, Lim originally learned to play the piano

12
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,517
like so many children, and also the violin,

13
00:01:04,517 --> 00:01:10,910
but when she was 11 her teachers encouraged her to start composing.

14
00:01:10,910 --> 00:01:14,663
She found this was her real strength,

15
00:01:14,663 --> 00:01:18,280
and she studied and later taught composition

16
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:22,170
both in Australia and in other countries.

17
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:28,994
As a composer, she has received commissions from numerous orchestras,

18
00:01:28,994 --> 00:01:33,770
other performers and festivals in several countries.

19
00:01:33,770 --> 00:01:41,915
Liza Lim's compositions are vibrant and full of energy, and she often explores Asian

20
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:45,860
and Australian Aboriginal cultural sources

21
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,314
including the native instrument, the didgeridoo:

22
00:01:48,314 --> 00:01:53,550
this is featured in a work called The Compass.

23
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:56,610
Her music is very expressive,

24
00:01:56,610 --> 00:02:00,920
so although it is complex

25
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:06,400
it has the power of connecting with audiences and performers alike.

26
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:13,624
In the festival we're going to give a semi-staged performance of The Oresteia.

27
00:02:13,624 --> 00:02:17,480
This is an opera in seven parts

28
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:23,070
based on the trilogy of ancient Greek tragedies by Aeschylus.

29
00:02:23,070 --> 00:02:33,360
Lim composed this when she was in her mid-20s, and she also wrote the text, along with Barrie Kosky.

30
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:40,240
It's performed by six singers, a dancer, and an orchestra that

31
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:43,423
as well as standard orchestral instruments,

32
00:02:43,423 --> 00:02:48,750
includes electric guitar, and a traditional Turkish stringed instrument.

33
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:56,595
Lim wrote that because the stories in the tragedies are not easy to tell,

34
00:02:56,595 --> 00:02:58,680
the sounds she creates are also disturbing

35
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:05,000
and they include breathing, sobbing, laughing and whistling.

36
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,200
The work lasts around 75 minutes,

37
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:19,400
and the rest of the concert will consist of orchestral works by the British composers Ralph Vaughan Williams and Frederick Delius.

38
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,598
Moving on now to our second concert,

39
00:03:23,598 --> 00:03:31,994
this will begin with instrumental music by British composers Benjamin Britten and Judith Weir.

40
00:03:31,994 --> 00:03:42,190
After the interval we'll go to Australia for a piece by Ross Edwards: The Tower of Remoteness.

41
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:47,403
According to Edwards, the inspiration for this piece came from nature,

42
00:03:47,403 --> 00:03:53,089
when he was sitting alone in the dry bed of a creek,

43
00:03:53,089 --> 00:03:59,250
overshadowed by the leaves of palm trees, listening to the birds and insects.

44
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:04,998
The Tower of Remoteness is scored for piano and clarinet.

45
00:04:04,998 --> 00:04:17,070
Edwards says he realised years after writing the piece that he had subconsciously modelled its opening phrase on a bird call.

46
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:21,993
Ross Edwards was born in 1943 in Sydney,

47
00:04:21,993 --> 00:04:27,600
Australia, and studied at the Sydney Conservatorium of Music

48
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,840
and the universities of Adelaide and Sydney.

49
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:35,140
He's well known in Australia

50
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:40,480
and in fact he's one of the country's most performed composers.

51
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:44,900
He's written a wide range of music

52
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:51,560
from symphonies and concertos to some composed specifically for children.

53
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:57,397
Edwards's music has been described as being 'deeply connected to Australia',

54
00:04:57,397 --> 00:05:07,480
and it can be regarded as a celebration of the diversity of cultures that Australia can be proud of.

55
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:17,109
The last of the three Australian composers to be represented in our festival is Carl Vine.

56
00:05:17,109 --> 00:05:24,330
Born in 1954, Vine, like Liza Lim, comes from Perth, Western Australia.

57
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:33,182
He took up the cornet at the age of five, switching to the piano five years later.

58
00:05:33,182 --> 00:05:39,430
However, he went to university to study physics, before changing to composition.

59
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:46,631
After graduating he moved to Sydney and worked as a freelance pianist and composer.

60
00:05:46,631 --> 00:05:53,317
Before long he had become prominent in Australia as a composer for dance,

61
00:05:53,317 --> 00:05:58,460
and in fact has written 25 scores of that type.

62
00:05:58,460 --> 00:06:10,810
In our third concert, Vine will be represented by his music for the flag hand-over ceremony of the Olympics held in 1996.

63
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:17,742
This seven-minute orchestral piece was of course heard by millions of people worldwide,

64
00:06:17,742 --> 00:06:24,940
and we'll hear it alongside works written by British composers Edward Elgar and

65
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,340
more recently, Thomas Adès.
